{"from": "text:josephus-ant-14-65", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-03-01-ninth-hour-prayer", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus: even under Pompey's siege the priests 'did still twice a-day, in the morning and about the ninth hour, offer their sacrifices on the altar' - the daily temple rite about the ninth hour, the same institution in the same place; it attests the temple hour, not that Peter and John went up at it."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-pesachim-5-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-03-01-ninth-hour-prayer", "grade": "C", "note": "The Mishnah fixes the daily afternoon offering between eight and a half and nine and a half hours - the ninth-hour rite Luke calls 'the hour of prayer'. Redacted c. 200 CE; it may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-5-201-206", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-03-02-beautiful-gate", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus describes one temple gate 'of Corinthian brass' that 'greatly excelled those that were only covered over with silver and gold', facing east over against the sanctuary - a renowned magnificent door of the temple that Luke's 'Beautiful' could name; Josephus never uses the name 'Beautiful'."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-5-201-206", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:ac-03-02-beautiful-gate", "note": "Whether the Corinthian-bronze gate is the inner Nicanor Gate or the outer Shushan (Eastern) Gate is disputed, and Acts' 'Beautiful Gate' has been identified with either; the fetched Wikipedia 'Beautiful Gate' article records that scholarly agreement on the identity 'met with little success'."}
{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-03-02-beautiful-gate", "grade": "B", "note": "Herod's temple stood in Jerusalem through the 30s CE with its courts and gates; it attests the setting of a temple door, not the gate's name."}
{"from": "artifact:nicanor-ossuary", "type": "attests", "to": "person:nicanor-of-alexandria", "grade": "A", "note": "The ossuary is inscribed 'Nicanor of Alexandria, who made the gates' - an excavated 1902 find naming the same Alexandrian door-maker whom Josephus and the Talmud describe as donor of the bronze temple doors."}
{"from": "artifact:nicanor-ossuary", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-03-02-beautiful-gate", "grade": "C", "note": "A first-century Alexandrian temple-door maker is independently attested, supporting the tradition of doors called Nicanor's; it cannot fix which gate Acts calls Beautiful, so it is graded C for the gate's identity."}
{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-03-11-solomons-porch", "grade": "B", "note": "Herod's temple stood in Jerusalem through the 30s CE with its porticoes; it attests the setting of a crowd gathering in a colonnade, not this particular gathering."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-220-221", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-03-11-solomons-porch", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus: Agrippa's workmen rebuilt the eastern cloisters, 'the work of king Solomon' - a colonnade of Herod's temple traditionally ascribed to Solomon, the same porch Acts 3:11 names; Josephus confirms the portico and its attribution, not this gathering."}
{"from": "claim:ac-03-11-solomons-porch", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-10-23-solomons-porch", "note": "The same colonnade as John 10:23 (Jesus walking in Solomon's porch); Acts 3:11 has a crowd running to Peter and John there after the healing."}
{"from": "claim:ac-03-13-delivered-denied-before-pilate", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:lk-23-16-chastise-release", "note": "Pilate's resolve to release Jesus; Luke 23:16 has Pilate proposing to chastise and release him - the same Passion detail, recalled in a speech here."}
{"from": "claim:ac-03-13-delivered-denied-before-pilate", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-27-01-02-delivered-to-pilate", "note": "The delivery of Jesus to Pilate by the chief priests and elders (Matthew 27:1-2); Acts 3:13 recalls it in Peter's speech."}
{"from": "claim:ac-03-14-murderer-requested", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-27-16-26-barabbas-released", "note": "The request for a murderer corresponds to the crowd choosing Barabbas at the Passover release (Matthew 27:16-26); Acts 3:14 does not name Barabbas."}
{"from": "claim:ac-03-22-prophet-like-moses", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:deuteronomy-18-15-18", "note": "Peter's quotation is Deuteronomy 18:15, 18 (biblical cross-reference, not evidence)."}
{"from": "claim:ac-03-25-offspring-blessing", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:genesis-22-18", "note": "Peter's quotation is Genesis 22:18 (biblical cross-reference, not evidence)."}
