{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-03-02-04-lame-beggar", "grade": "B", "note": "Herod's temple stood in Jerusalem through the 30s CE with gates through which worshippers entered daily; it attests the setting of alms-seeking at a temple door, not this man or his laying there."}
{"from": "artifact:pilate-stone", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-03-13-delivered-denied-before-pilate", "grade": "B", "note": "The Caesarea inscription names '[Po]ntius Pilatus, [praef]ectus Iuda[e]ae' - the governor before whom Acts 3:13 says Jesus was delivered up and denied; the delivery itself it does not attest."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-annals-15-44", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-03-13-delivered-denied-before-pilate", "grade": "B", "note": "Tacitus: Christus 'suffered the extreme penalty during the reign of Tiberius at the hands of one of our procurators, Pontius Pilatus' - the Passion outcome of the delivery Peter recalls; not the denial or Pilate's resolve to release."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-63-64-testimonium", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-03-13-delivered-denied-before-pilate", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus (Testimonium): Pilate condemned Jesus to the cross after the leading men accused him - the same Passion sequence in the same period; the Christian interpolations in the passage are disputed, and Josephus never mentions Pilate's resolve to release."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-63-64-testimonium", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-03-15-god-raised-witnesses", "grade": "D", "note": "The disputed Testimonium reports 'for he appeared to them alive again the third day' - a report that Jesus' followers claimed appearances, but the clause's authenticity is disputed and it does not attest the apostles' witness status. (Quote trimmed by reviewer 10 to what the fetched excerpt carries.)"}
{"from": "site:cave-of-nicanor", "type": "attests", "to": "person:nicanor-of-alexandria", "grade": "B", "note": "The elaborate burial complex on Mount Scopus contains the ossuary inscribed for Nicanor the door maker; the fetched page describes the burial cave containing Nicanor's ossuary as typical of the Second Temple period - the documented tomb of the man. (Note corrected by reviewer 10: the page does not carry the 'dated by plan, style and finds' wording.)"}
